   Yoann and Caussimont, Gerard and Chetouane, Elias and Carriburu, Jules
   Chiffard and Cornette, Pierre and Delestrade, Anne and De Backer, Nina
   and Dispan, Lucie and Le Barh, Maden and Duhayer, Jeanne and Elder,
   Jean-Francois and Fanjul, Jean-Baptiste and Fonderflick, Jocelyn and
   Froustey, Nicolas and Garel, Mathieu and Gaudry, William and Gerard,
   Agathe and Gimenez, Olivier and Hemery, Arzhela and Hemon, Audrey and
   Jullien, Jean-Michel and Knitter, Daniel and Malafosse, Isabelle and
   Marginean, Mircea and Menard, Louise and Ouvrier, Alice and Pariset,
   Gwennaelle and Prunet, Vincent and Rabault, Julien and Randon, Malory
   and Raulet, Yann and Regnier, Antoine and Ribiere, Romain and Ricci,
   Jean-Claude and Ruette, Sandrine and Schneylin, Yann and Sentilles,
   Jerome and Siefert, Nathalie and Smith, Bethany and Terpereau, Guillaume
   and Touchet, Pierrick and Thuiller, Wilfried and Uzal, Antonio and
   Vautrain, Valentin and Vimal, Ruppert and Weber, Julian and Spataro,
   Bruno and Miele, Vincent and Chamaille-Jammes, Simon},
Title = {The DeepFaune initiative: a collaborative effort towards the automatic
   identification of European fauna in camera trap images},
Journal = {EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF WILDLIFE RESEARCH},
Year = {2023},
Volume = {69},
Number = {6},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Camera traps have revolutionized how ecologists monitor wildlife, but
   their full potential is realized only when the hundreds of thousands of
   collected images can be readily classified with minimal human
   intervention. Deep learning classification models have allowed
   extraordinary progress towards this end, but trained models remain rare
   and are only now emerging for European fauna. We report on the first
   milestone of the DeepFaune initiative (https://www.deepfaune.cnrs.fr), a
   large-scale collaboration between more than 50 partners involved in
   wildlife research, conservation and management in France. We developed a
   classification model trained to recognize 26 species or higher-level
   taxa that are common in Europe, with an emphasis on mammals. The
   classification model achieved 0.97 validation accuracy and often > 0.95
   precision and recall for many classes. These performances were generally
   higher than 0.90 when tested on independent out-of-sample datasets for
   which we used image redundancy contained in sequences of images. We
   implemented our model in a software to classify images stored locally on
   a personal computer, so as to provide a free, user-friendly, and
   high-performance tool for wildlife practitioners to automatically
   classify camera trap images. The DeepFaune initiative is an ongoing
   project, with new partners joining regularly, which allows us to
   continuously add new species to the classification model.},
